                                            },
                                            {
                                                "box": {
                                                    "id": "obj-35",
                                                    "linecount": 57,
                                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1120.0 ],
                                                    "text": "================================================================================\nrev_velvet_grain — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nA reverb built from velvet noise: sparse random +1/-1 clicks instead of dense\ndelay-line echoes. Four click patterns (97, 113, 128 and 140 ms long) each\nrecirculate through their own lowpass loop, and their signs are mixed so the\nleft and right tails are uncorrelated. Each click also gets a random width; the\nwider ones are darker (dark velvet noise).\n\nSource: Prawda, Schlecht & Välimäki, DAFx-22 Paper 28 §2 Eq. 1, §3.3\n(interleaved velvet-noise reverberator, orthogonal sign mixing); Fagerström et\nal., DAFx-22 Paper 31 §3 (dark velvet noise: random pulse widths as running-sum\nfilters); Roberts et al., DAFx-23 Paper 57 §2.2 (low density sounds rough).\n\nThe point of this one is texture. At high density it is a smooth plain tail;\nturn grain_hz down and the tail breaks into audible crackle, like a dusty\nsampler reverb under a trip-hop loop. 100% wet, no dry/wet.\n\nPARAMETERS (6 + output gain)\n\n1. predelay_ms — 0…250 ms, default 10 ms\n   Gap before the tail starts.\n\n2. decay_s — 0.2…10 s, default 2 s\n   RT60 of the recirculating loops (the loop lowpass shortens the top end a\n   little more).\n\n3. grain_hz — 40…1200 pulses/s, default 400\n   Click density. 900–1200 = smooth; 200–500 = grainy; below ~120 you hear\n   individual ticks, a crackly lo-fi rattle. Level stays about constant.\n\n4. damp_hz — 500…16000 Hz, default 5000 Hz\n   Darkness. Sets both the widest click width and the lowpass inside the loops,\n   so low values give a soft, dark, woolly grain and high values a bright,\n   spitty one.\n\n5. lowcut_hz — 20…600 Hz, default 150 Hz\n   Two one-pole highpasses before the reverb. Keep it at 120–250 Hz to leave\n   the sub dry.\n\n6. width — 0…1, default 1.0\n   Mid/side width. 0 = mono, 1 = the two decorrelated outputs as they are.\n\n7. out_gain_db — -24…+12 dB, default 0 dB\n   Final output trim. Does not count toward the 8-param limit.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: stereo audio (summed to mono to excite the tail).\n- out1 / out2: stereo wet only.\n\nNOTES\n- The click patterns are deterministic (fixed seeds); changing grain_hz\n  rebuilds them, so moving it while a tail rings can tick.\n- Level is normalised against the loop gain, so long decays are not louder.\n- No limiter, no dry path.\n",
                                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                                }
                                            }
