                                                    "text": "param bessel_mode 1 @min 0 @max 1",
                                                    "varname": "bessel_mode"
                                                }
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "box": {
                                                    "id": "obj-24",
                                                    "linecount": 50,
                                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 675.0 ],
                                                    "text": "================================================================================\n06_asymmetric_delay_adfm — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nOVERVIEW\nImplements Asymmetric Delay-Based Adaptive Frequency Modulation (ASDFM1 & ASDFM2) from Lazzarini, Timoney, and Lysaght (DAFx-08, Section 2, Eq. 3 & 4). The full stereo tuba signal acts as a multi-harmonic carrier passed through a cubic-interpolated variable delay line modulated at a ratio of the tracked pitch, then ring-modulated by an exponentially mapped cosine wave normalized by a rational approximation of the Modified Bessel function I0. Displaces sideband energy above or below each brass harmonic to sculpt synthetic formants without sub-bass mud.\n\nINLETS & OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: Stereo Audio Input (Full Tuba Carrier)\n- in3: Tracked Fundamental Pitch in Hz (from fluid.pitch~; falls back to internal zero-crossing tracker if <= 0)\n- out1 / out2: Stereo Asymmetric Adaptive FM Output\n- Level: the I0-normalised exponential envelope keeps the average gain near unity but is peaky: on the sousa_mic.aif take (-32 dB RMS, -14 dBFS peak in) the defaults give about -28 dB RMS and -5 dBFS peaks (offline render of the real RNBO code). About 2 samples of latency plus the modulated delay.\n\nPARAMETERS (4)\n\n1. cm_ratio\n   - Range: 0.25 to 4.00 (Carrier-to-Modulator Frequency Ratio c:m, where f_m = f_0 / cm_ratio)\n   - Taper: Linear ratio (1.0 = fundamental-locked harmonic sidebands; 2.0 = sub-octave sidebands; 3.0 = twelfth down; non-integer values like 2.45 create metallic multiphonic growls).\n   - Function: Sets the pitch-tracked modulator frequency relative to the tuba's fundamental.\n\n2. mod_index\n   - Range: 0.0 to 6.0 (FM Index k)\n   - Taper: Power curve (x^1.3) for smooth low-index warmth and aggressive high-index spectral expansion. Delay depth is automatically scaled by 1 / (pi * f0) so modulation brightness remains uniform across the tuba's entire register.\n   - Function: Controls the bandwidth and number of sidebands generated around every tuba partial.\n\n3. symmetry_r\n   - Range: -1.0 to +1.0 (maps exponentially to Palamin symmetry factor r = 4^symmetry_r in [0.25, 4.00])\n   - Taper: Exponential octave-symmetric taper centered at 0.0 (r = 1.0, classic symmetric FM). Positive values (r > 1.0) shift sideband energy into the upper spectrum (bright formant boost without muddying sub-bass); negative values (r < 1.0) shift energy into lower sub-harmonics.\n   - Function: Dislocates the center of spectral symmetry above or below each carrier harmonic. Works in both bessel_mode settings (earlier versions dropped the sign of the phase index in ASDFM2 mode, so +x and -x sounded identical there).\n\n4. bessel_mode\n   - Range: 0.0 to 1.0 (0.0 = ASDFM1 Standard Bessel Jn, 1.0 = ASDFM2 Modified Bessel In)\n   - Taper: Linear crossfade between Eq. 3 and Eq. 4 coefficient mappings.\n   - Function: Morphs between standard oscillating Bessel sidebands (0.0) and monotonic Modified Bessel sidebands (1.0), which guarantee that sideband n never exceeds sideband n-1, creating clean, undistorted \"moving formants.\"\n",
                                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                                }
                                            }
